[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is campus DNS on srv1 (BIND/named). Laptop1’s L2/L3 path is healthy: it has 10.6.0.100/24 with default gateway 10.6.0.1, can ARP the gateway (MAC 3a:16:00:81:b9:b2 on dswa2) and can reach other subnets by IP (successful pings to 10.1.0.100/101). However, name resolution fails: Laptop1 is configured to use 10.5.0.4 (srv1) for DNS, and nslookup to 10.5.0.4 returns “REFUSED” with “recursion not available.” Direct evidence on srv1 shows named is authoritative-only with recursion disabled and no forwarders (named.conf: “recursion no; dnssec-validation no;” plus only the local “jeremysitlab.com” zone). This causes typical web/app lookups (e.g., www.google.com) to be refused, so the OS shows “connected Wi‑Fi” but nothing loads.

Localization: srv1’s bind9 service (10.5.0.4:53) and clients across the campus pointing at it (e.g., Laptop1 /etc/resolv.conf). The wireless gateway itself (10.6.0.1 on dswa2) is up and routing.

Root cause: srv1 is configured as an authoritative-only DNS server with recursion disabled and no upstream forwarders, so clients’ recursive DNS queries are refused.

Corrective fix: On srv1, enable recursion for internal clients and add reachable forwarders, e.g.:
- In named.conf/options: recursion yes; allow-recursion { 10.0.0.0/8; }; forwarders { <ISP or public resolvers>; };
- Reload bind (systemctl reload bind9).
Alternatively, update DHCP/Wi‑Fi to hand out a resolver that provides recursion. After this, clients will resolve names and browsing will work. === END ===